\paragraph{Identity \eqref{eq:balance}.}
Let $G=\Wt\Sigma_{tc}-\Pred\Wc\Sigma_c$ and $A=\Pred^\top G\Wc^\top$.
From \eqref{eq:flowW}--\eqref{eq:flowP}, $\tfrac{d}{dt}\Pred^\top\Pred=\alpha(A+A^\top)-2\alpha\lambda_P\Pred^\top\Pred$ and $\tfrac{d}{dt}\Wc\Wc^\top=A+A^\top-2\lambda_W\Wc\Wc^\top$.
For $\alpha=1$ and $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$ the difference $D=\Pred^\top\Pred-\Wc\Wc^\top$ obeys $\dot D=-2\lambda D$; for $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=0$, $\tfrac{d}{dt}(\alpha^{-1}\Pred^\top\Pred-\Wc\Wc^\top)=0$.
Numerically (\texttt{code/checks/balance\_identity.py}), the deviation from \eqref{eq:balance} along the flow is at most $\BalanceDevFlow$ for $\alpha=1$ with $\|\Wc\|$ up to $\BalanceMaxNormW$, and the identity fails, as it should, for $\alpha=0.5$ and $\alpha=2$ (relative deviations $\BalanceDevAlphaHalf$ and $\BalanceDevAlphaTwo$).
$\square$

\paragraph{\Cref{prop:reduction}.}
Substitute the ansatz.
Then $G=\sum_k(c_k\bar a_k-p_k\sigma_ka_k)\tilde u_ku_k^\top$, so $\Pred^\top G-\lambda_W\Wc=\sum_k\big[p_k(c_k\bar a_k-p_k\sigma_ka_k)-\lambda_Wa_k\big]\tilde u_ku_k^\top$, $G\Wc^\top-\lambda_P\Pred=\sum_k\big[(c_k\bar a_k-p_k\sigma_ka_k)a_k-\lambda_Pp_k\big]\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top-\lambda_Pp_\perp\big(I-\sum_k\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top\big)$, and $\kappa(\Wc-\Wt)=\kappa\sum_k(a_k-\bar a_k)\tilde u_ku_k^\top$.
The vector field is tangent to the ansatz manifold and reduces on it to \eqref{eq:reduced}; the initial condition lies on the manifold, and uniqueness of solutions of the (locally Lipschitz) flow gives the claim.
With $\bar a_k=a_k$ the first equation factorises as $\dot a_k=a_kg_k(p_k)$.
In E1, the reduced flow integrated with the same step as full-matrix gradient descent classifies the survival of every direction in the orthogonal-initialisation runs as gradient descent does (\EoneReducedModeAccOrth). $\square$

\paragraph{\Cref{prop:fixed}.}
(a) At $(a,p)=(0,0)$ the Jacobian of \eqref{eq:growth} is $\mathrm{diag}(-\lambda_W,-\alpha\lambda_P)$, because the drive $pc_ka$ and the data term $(c_k-p\sigma_k)a^2$ are of second order there.
(b) A fixed point with $a\neq0$ needs $g_k(p)=0$, so $p=p_\pm^k$, which requires $c_k^2\ge4\lambda_W\sigma_k$, and $\dot p=0$, so $a^2=\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$.
For $c_k>0$, $0<p_-^k\le p_+^k<c_k/\sigma_k$ when $\lambda_W>0$, so $a^2>0$; the case $c_k<0$ is the mirror image under $p\mapsto-p$.
At such a point $\partial_a\dot a=g_k(p)=0$, $\partial_p\dot a=ag_k'(p)$, $\partial_a\dot p=2\alpha(c_k-p\sigma_k)a$ and $\partial_p\dot p=-\alpha(\sigma_ka^2+\lambda_P)$.
Hence $\mathrm{tr}\,J=-\alpha(\sigma_ka^2+\lambda_P)<0$ and $\det J=-2\alpha a^2(c_k-p\sigma_k)\,g_k'(p)$.
For $c_k>0$, $g_k'(p_-^k)=\sqrt{c_k^2-4\lambda_W\sigma_k}>0$ gives $\det J<0$, a saddle, and $g_k'(p_+^k)<0$ gives $\det J>0$ with negative trace, a stable point.
The rank bound follows because a direction without a non-collapsed fixed point has $a_k\to0$ (\cref{prop:reduction} decouples the directions).
(c) With $\lambda_P=0$, $\dot p=0$ and $a\neq0$ give $p=c_k/\sigma_k$, and $g_k(c_k/\sigma_k)=-\lambda_W\neq0$, so $\dot a\neq0$. $\square$

\paragraph{\Cref{cor:alpha1}.}
Under the ansatz, the $\tilde u_k\tilde u_k^\top$ component of $\Pred^\top\Pred-\Wc\Wc^\top$ is $p_k^2-a_k^2$, and \eqref{eq:balance} gives $p_k^2-a_k^2=e^{-2\lambda t}(p_0^2-a_k(0)^2)$.
Since $p_k(0)=p_0>0$ and $p_k$ can vanish only where $a_k$ does, $p_k$ is the positive root.
In the balanced case $p_k=|a_k|$, and for $a>0$ the scalar equation $\dot a=a\,g_k(a)$ has the fixed points $0$, $p_-^k$ and $p_+^k$; $a$ decreases to $0$ from $(0,p_-^k)$ and converges to $p_+^k$ from $(p_-^k,\infty)$.
For small $\lambda$, $p_-^k=\lambda/c_k+O(\lambda^2)$. $\square$

\paragraph{Integrated rate \eqref{eq:F}.}
With $p(t)=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}$, $dt=-dq/(\alpha\lambda_Pq)$, so $\int_0^tg_k(p(s))\,ds=(\alpha\lambda_P)^{-1}\int_{p(t)}^{p_0}\big(c_k-q\sigma_k-\lambda_W/q\big)\,dq=(\alpha\lambda_P)^{-1}\big[c_k(p_0-p)-\tfrac{\sigma_k}2(p_0^2-p^2)\big]-\lambda_Wt$, using $\log(p_0/p)=\alpha\lambda_Pt$.
$F_k$ increases exactly while $g_k(p(t))>0$, so on $\{p\ge p_-^k\}$ it is maximal at $p=p_-^k$ when $p_-^k<p_0\le p_+^k$; when $p_0>p_+^k$ it first decreases, and its maximum is at $p_-^k$ or at $t=0$, whichever is larger.

\paragraph{Leading order \eqref{eq:lamrace}.}
Evaluate \eqref{eq:race} at $p=p_-^k$ with $\lambda_W=\lambda_P=\lambda$ and multiply by $\alpha\lambda$.
For small $\lambda$, $p_-^k=\lambda/c_k+O(\lambda^2)$, so $c_k(p_0-p_-^k)-\tfrac{\sigma_k}{2}(p_0^2-(p_-^k)^2)=h_k(p_0)-\lambda+O(\lambda^2)$, $\lambda\log(p_0/p_-^k)=\lambda\log(c_kp_0/\lambda)+O(\lambda^2)$ and $\tfrac12\log\big(\lambda p_-^k/(c_k-p_-^k\sigma_k)\big)=\log(\lambda/c_k)+O(\lambda)$.
Collecting terms and dropping $O(\lambda)$ against the logarithms gives
\[
h_k(p_0)\gtrsim\alpha\lambda\Big[\log\frac{1}{a_0}+\log\frac{\lambda}{c_k}+\frac1\alpha\log\frac{c_kp_0}{\lambda}\Big],
\]
and for $\alpha=1$ the logarithms combine to $\log(p_0/a_0)$, which is \eqref{eq:lamrace}.
For $\alpha\ne1$ the remainder $(1-1/\alpha)\log(\lambda/c_k)+\alpha^{-1}\log p_0$ is of the same size as $\log(1/a_0)$ at the weight decays of interest, so the leading order is not a usable threshold there and we evaluate \eqref{eq:race} directly. $\square$

\paragraph{EMA rate (\cref{sec:heuristics}).}
With an EMA target of rate $\kappa=(1-\tau)/\eta$ and fixed gain $p$, the linear system $\dot a=pc\,\bar a-(p^2\sigma+\lambda_W)a$, $\dot{\bar a}=\kappa(a-\bar a)$ has characteristic polynomial $f(r)=(r+p^2\sigma+\lambda_W)(r+\kappa)-pc\kappa$.
Its determinant is $f(0)=-\kappa\,g(p)$, so the system has a growing solution if and only if $g(p)>0$, for every $\kappa>0$.
Moreover $f(g(p))=pc\,g(p)$.
For $pc>0$ and $g>0$, $f(0)<0<f(g)$ puts the largest root in $(0,g)$; for $g<0$, $f(g)<0<f(0)$ puts it in $(g,0)$.
In both cases the growth rate under EMA has the sign of $g$ and a smaller magnitude. $\square$

\paragraph{Bernoulli masks (\cref{sec:heuristics}).}
Each coordinate enters the context independently with probability $q$, and the target is the complement.
Then $G_{ij}=q^2$ for $i\neq j$ and $G_{ii}=q$, and $F_{ij}=q(1-q)$ for $i\neq j$ and $F_{ii}=0$.
With $\Sigma_{ii}=\bar\sigma$, $\Sigma_c=q^2\Sigma+q(1-q)\bar\sigma I$ and $\Sigma_{tc}=q(1-q)(\Sigma-\bar\sigma I)$, which share the eigenvectors of $\Sigma$ and have the eigenvalues stated in \cref{sec:heuristics}. $\square$

