\paragraph{Two known limits.}
Without the stop-gradient, under \cref{ass:aligned}(i) with $\Sigma_t u_k=\sigma_k^tu_k$, the near-collapse rate of direction $k$ is $2pc_k-p^2\sigma_k-\sigma_k^t-\lambda_W\le c_k^2/\sigma_k-\sigma_k^t-\lambda_W\le-\lambda_W$, since $c_k^2\le\sigma_k\sigma_k^t$ by positive semidefiniteness of the joint covariance: no direction grows.
Without a predictor ($\Pred\equiv I$), $g_k=c_k-\sigma_k-\lambda_W$.
Since $c_k^2\le\sigma_k\sigma_k^t$, no direction grows whenever the target's marginal does not exceed the context's, $\sigma_k^t\le\sigma_k$, in particular for views with equal marginals; a trainable predictor can shrink $p$ into $(p_-^k,p_+^k)\subset(0,c_k/\sigma_k)$, which a fixed $\Pred=I$ cannot.
Both statements are known in substance \citep{tian2021understanding,halvagal2023implicit}; in the race picture they say that the stop-gradient and the predictor are what make a positive net rate possible.
With masks the second one can fail, because the target is the masked part and its marginal can exceed the context's.
For the Bernoulli masks of \eqref{eq:bernoulli} with target fraction $r=1-q$, $c_k-\sigma_k=q\big[(r-q)\hat\Sigma_k-2r\bar\sigma\big]$, which is positive for $r>1/2$ and $\hat\Sigma_k>2r\bar\sigma/(2r-1)$; with $\Pred$ fixed such a direction has no fixed point and grows without bound in the linear model.
``No predictor implies collapse'' therefore holds for equal-marginal views but not for heavy Bernoulli masking.

\paragraph{Beyond the aligned case.}
Without \cref{ass:aligned}, the near-collapse dynamics are still linear in $(\Wc,\Wt)$, with $\Pred(t)=e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}\Pred_0$.
The general form of the criterion integrates this $dn$-dimensional linear system and counts the singular directions of $\Wc$ that reach their capture amplitude while they still grow.
For aligned statistics and $\Pred_0=p_0I$ it reduces to \cref{def:race}.
We state it because it is the form needed for non-commuting statistics and random predictors, and test it in the held-out experiments (\cref{sec:experiments}).

\paragraph{Random and wide predictors.}
For a non-normal $\Pred_0$, \cref{ass:aligned}(ii) fails and the growth of data direction $k$ is governed by the matrix $c_k\Pred(t)^\top-\sigma_k\Pred(t)^\top\Pred(t)-\lambda_W$.
A predictor direction $v$ can drive growth only if $c_k\,v^\top\Pred_0v>\sigma_k\|\Pred_0v\|^2+\lambda_W$.
For a Gaussian $\Pred_0$ with entries of variance $s^2/d$, $v^\top\Pred_0v\sim s/\sqrt d$ while $\|\Pred_0v\|^2\sim s^2$, so the prediction is that the scale $s$, not the width $d$, sets which directions can grow; the rank is the matching rank of \cref{sec:setup}, computed from the general form of the criterion.

\paragraph{Details of the heuristic predictions.}
Without weight decay the gain does not drift and direction $k$ is lost for every $p_0\ge c_k/\sigma_k$, so a predictor initialised at or above the largest least-squares gain, $p_0\ge\max_kc_k/\sigma_k$, collapses the representation completely although every direction with $c_k>0$ has a non-empty window.
With $\lambda_P=0$, $\alpha$ has no effect near collapse; with $\lambda_P>0$, $\alpha$ shortens the clock $1/(\alpha\lambda_P)$, but the rank does not depend on $\alpha$ and $\lambda_P$ only through their product, because the window and the encoder's decay $\lambda_Wt$ in \eqref{eq:F} do not scale with $\alpha$; with depth the pre-registered criterion uses only the clock $L\alpha\lambda_P$, so that rank$(L,\alpha)=$ rank$(1,L\alpha)$ for it, and this over-predicts the rank (\cref{sec:heldout}).
A very slow target, $\kappa$ small compared with the growth rate, should lower the rank in both EMA regimes, and since only $\kappa$ during the race enters, a schedule $\tau_0\to1$ should give the rank of a constant $\tau_0$. Neither is tested: E3 registers for the EMA rate only the null ``no change beyond seed spread'', and it has no constant-$\tau_0$ configuration; that null fails, with fewer directions for a faster target (\cref{app:details}).
For Bernoulli masks \eqref{eq:bernoulli} gives $c_k^2/\sigma_k=q(1-q)^2(\hat\Sigma_k-\bar\sigma)^2/\big(q\hat\Sigma_k+(1-q)\bar\sigma\big)$.

\paragraph{Depth and capture (post hoc).}
This correction was derived after the E1b depth failure and then tested on new statistics in E1c (\cref{sec:heldout}, \cref{app:details}).
For a per-mode gain $p=q^L$ of $L$ identity-initialised factors, each a separate parameter $q$ with learning-rate ratio $\alpha$ and weight decay $\lambda_P$, $\partial\ell/\partial q=q^{L-1}\partial\ell/\partial p$, so $\dot q=\alpha\big[q^{L-1}(c_k\bar a-p\sigma_ka)a-\lambda_Pq\big]$ and
\begin{equation*}
\dot p=\alpha L\big[p^{2-2/L}(c_k\bar a-p\sigma_ka)a-\lambda_Pp\big].
\end{equation*}
The factor $L$ sets the clock $L\alpha\lambda_P$ and drops out of the nullcline: with $\bar a=a$, capture needs $a^2\ge\lambda_P\,p^{2/L-1}/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$ instead of $\lambda_Pp/(c_k-p\sigma_k)$.
The ratio of the two thresholds, $p^{2/L-2}$, exceeds one for $p<1$ and grows without bound as $p\to0$: near $p_-^k\approx\lambda_W/c_k$, where capture is decided, a deep predictor needs a much larger amplitude than the depth-1 nullcline assumes, the direction of the measured over-prediction; $F_k$ is unchanged.
The corrected criterion replaces the capture term of \eqref{eq:race} by this nullcline.

\paragraph{Frozen target.}
A frozen target ($\kappa=0$, $\bar w=a_0$) removes the feedback, so there is no exponential growth: $\dot a=pc_ka_0-(p^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W)a$ and $\dot p=\alpha\big(c_ka_0a-p\sigma_ka^2\big)-\alpha\lambda_Pp$.
Without weight decay the encoder regresses onto the random target, $a\to c_ka_0/(p\sigma_k)$, and nothing collapses.
With weight decay the Jacobian at $(a,p)=(0,0)$ has determinant $\alpha(\lambda_W\lambda_P-c_k^2a_0^2)$ and trace $-\lambda_W-\alpha\lambda_P$, and at $a=pc_ka_0/(p^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W)$ the gain obeys $\dot p=\alpha p\big[\lambda_Wc_k^2a_0^2/(p^2\sigma_k+\lambda_W)^2-\lambda_P\big]$; the collapsed point is therefore stable, and it is the only stationary point unless $c_k|a_0|>\sqrt{\lambda_W\lambda_P}$, which fails at a small initial scale: a frozen target collapses under weight decay.

\paragraph{Initial scale and optimiser.}
The initial amplitude enters only through $\log(1/a_0)$, the distance the direction must cover, so larger initialisations keep more directions, with a logarithmic sensitivity.
Decoupled weight decay in AdamW keeps the race clock, $p\propto(1-\eta\lambda_P)^{\text{step}}$, but Adam normalises the predictor's data gradient, which is of order $a^2$ near collapse.
Once that gradient exceeds Adam's $\epsilon$, the gain moves by order $\eta$ per step towards $c_k/\sigma_k$ even at tiny amplitude, so we expect the rank under Adam to depend less on $p_0$ and more on weight decay, through \cref{prop:fixed}; this is a stated departure from the gradient-flow analysis.

