\paragraph{E3: set-up.}
The encoder is a ViT with patch size 4 (64 tokens), width 128, depth 4 and 4 heads; the predictor a 3-block transformer of width 64; batch 256, 195 steps per epoch, 3 seeds (0--2) per configuration, CIFAR-10 training images~\citep{krizhevsky2009cifar}.
Arm P uses AdamW (learning rate $3\cdot10^{-4}$, warmup and cosine), weight decay rising from $0.04$ to $0.4$, an EMA target with $\tau$ from $0.996$ to $1$, target LayerNorm and four target blocks (scale $0.15$--$0.2$) with a context of scale $0.85$--$1$.
Arm T uses SGD with momentum $0.9$ and constant learning rate $0.05$, a copy target with stop-gradient, no LayerNorm in the predictor or on the target, and constant weight decay on every parameter ($\lambda=0.01$ at baseline).
All schedules are defined over 50 epochs and each sweep run is its first 20 (3900 steps), so counts are horizon counts.
The count is the number of eigenvalues of the centred online-token covariance on the 10\,000 test images that are at least $10^{-3}$ of the largest; it is \EthreeInitCount{} at initialisation, and a run is dimensionally collapsed if its count falls below half of that. The probe is a ridge linear classifier on the pooled online features (ridge chosen on a validation split of the training images), scored on the test split.
The horizon rule (\texttt{code/e3\_grid.py}) takes, per eigendirection of that covariance at step 0, the drive, self-prediction and decoupled-decay rates, scales them with the run's learning-rate and weight-decay schedules (the gradient part by $1/(1-\text{momentum})$ under SGD), integrates the log-eigenvalue over the horizon with growth saturating at the top initial eigenvalue, and applies the same $10^{-3}$ threshold; it fits nothing.
It predicts no Arm P transition for $\lambda\le10^2$, so the registered fallback $\lambda=\EthreePwdlargeVal$ was run there.
Predictor learning rate and every target variant have the baseline's step-0 forces, so the rule predicts no change for them; their directions come from the linear theory (\cref{sec:heuristics}).
The grid (\cref{fig:e3-factors}), the predictions and criteria (\cref{tab:e3}) were committed before the \EthreeSweepRuns{} sweep runs, and a follow-up of \EthreeFollowConfigs{} configurations (\EthreeFollowRuns{} runs: predictor interventions on Arm T and the copy target, and three full-length runs with a linear probe) before its runs (\texttt{results/e3/prediction.json}, \texttt{prediction\_followup.json}).

\paragraph{E3: results.}
Arm T's rank correlation rests on its weight-decay sweep (post hoc, not registered: Spearman \EthreeRankTwd{} over the \EthreeRankTwdN{} weight-decay configurations, \EthreeRankTother{} over the other \EthreeRankTwdN{}, five of which share the baseline's rule count); the rule misses the predictor-init $\times3$ configuration (rule \EthreeTpinitPred{}, measured \EthreeTpinitMeas{}), and the frozen target, registered as not collapsing, collapses in Arm T (\EthreeTfrozen{}) as its decayed baseline does (\EthreeTbase{}), but not in Arm P (\EthreePfrozen{}); \cref{app:more} predicts collapse of a frozen target under weight decay only from a small initial scale, which E3 does not have.
In Arm P the rule predicts \EthreePbasePred{} directions for nearly every configuration, because under AdamW the step-0 update is a sign update, while the measured counts vary: a slower predictor (learning rate $\times0.1$: \EthreePplrLow{}) or a smaller predictor initialisation ($\times0.1$: \EthreePpinitLow{}, seeds \EthreePpinitLowSeeds{}) lowers the count, depth does not change it monotonically (\EthreePdepth{} for depths 1, 3, 6), the four mask settings give \EthreePmasks{} (one target block, context $0.4$--$0.5$, target scale $0.3$--$0.4$ and $0.05$--$0.1$) against \EthreePbase{} at baseline, so all four registered nulls fail; weight decay leaves it unchanged between $\lambda=0$ and $0.04$ (\EthreePwdzero{}, \EthreePwdlow{}) and lowers it only at the fallback $\lambda=\EthreePwdlargeVal$, to \EthreePwdlarge{}, so Arm P's weight-decay sign (\cref{tab:e3}) is not a graded effect.
Of the registered directions, \EthreeSignedHeld{} of \EthreeSigned{} signed pairs and \EthreeNullsHeld{} of \EthreeNulls{} nulls hold.
A faster target lowers the count (\EthreePema{} for copy and $\tau=0.9,0.99,0.999$; this sign is post hoc), opposite to the slow-target regime of \cref{sec:heuristics}, which E3 does not reach.
Without stop-gradient the total variance falls to \EthreePnosgTv{} (P) and \EthreeTnosgTv{} (T) of its initial value by epoch 20: scale collapse under way but not complete, as the criterion requires.
Interventions (\cref{fig:e3-interventions}a): the theory's prescriptions for Arm T, a slower predictor to rescue $\lambda=0.03$ and $0.1$ and a faster one to break $\lambda=10^{-3}$, move the count in the predicted direction in \EthreeCfourTheoryDir{} of 3 (the faster predictor raises it, \EthreeBreakMeas{} against \EthreeBreakParent{}); the rule's prescriptions, predictor initialisation $\times3$ and $\times0.3$, do so in \EthreeCfourRuleDir{} of 3, but the predicted rescues to \EthreeRescuePred{} directions end at \EthreeRescueMeas{}; in Arm P a copy target without weight decay or with a slower predictor gains directions (\EthreeCfourPDir{} of 2).
At 50 epochs (\cref{fig:e3-interventions}b) the counts are \EthreeFullCounts{} (rule \EthreeFullPred{}) for Arm P's baseline and Arm T at $\lambda=0.01$ and $0.03$, so the epoch-20 counts are not final, and the probe accuracies are \EthreeProbePbase{}, \EthreeProbeTlo{} and \EthreeProbeThi{}: the registered probe criterion compares two collapsing Arm T configurations, both below the random-initialisation probe (\EthreeProbeInit{}); it supports only ``more collapse, lower probe'' within Arm T, and the one healthy configuration uses the other optimiser.
\Cref{fig:e3-dynamics} shows the counts and the variance over the 20 epochs. As a diagnostic, not registered, the number of directions with net growth at step 800 has Spearman \EthreeDiagT{} (T) and \EthreeDiagP{} (P) with the final count.

