\paragraph{Reproduction and compute.}
All linear experiments use NumPy (\EoneNumpy) on CPU and population statistics, so they have no data split and no dataset; seeds draw the initialisation.
E1 is \texttt{python3 code/e1\_linear.py results/e1} followed by \texttt{code/e1\_analyze.py}, and its \EoneRuns{} runs took \EoneSeconds{}\,s on 8 CPUs.
E1b is \texttt{code/e1b.py run results/e1b}, which reads the committed predictions in \texttt{results/e1b/prediction.json}, followed by \texttt{code/e1b\_analyze.py}; its \EoneBRuns{} runs took \EoneBSeconds{}\,s on \EoneBNproc{} CPUs.
Each result file records the command that made it; \texttt{code/README.md} lists the scripts, and the pinned dependencies are in \texttt{code/requirements.txt}.
E1c is \texttt{code/e1c.py predict}, \texttt{run} and \texttt{analyze} on \texttt{results/e1c} (\EoneCRuns{} runs, \EoneCSeconds{}\,s).
Every number in the text is generated from these files by \texttt{paper/tools/make\_numbers.py}.
E2 is \texttt{code/e2.py predict}, \texttt{run} and \texttt{analyze} on \texttt{results/e2} (\EtwoRuns{} runs, \EtwoSeconds{}\,s on 8 CPUs; synthetic data drawn from the seeds), and the rank-over-time diagnostic is \texttt{code/checks/e2\_rank\_over\_time.py}.
E3 is \texttt{code/e3\_grid.py predict}, then the training script \texttt{code/e3\_ijepa.py} (PyTorch 2.5.1) for every run, then \texttt{e3\_grid.py analyze} and \texttt{analyze-followup} on the run directories, which are released as an archive with the results; the \EthreeSweepRuns{}+\EthreeFollowRuns{} runs took \EthreeRunHours{} run-hours (\EthreeVruns{} runs, \EthreeVhours{}\,h, on V100 GPUs and \EthreeGtxRuns{}, \EthreeGtxHours{}\,h, on GTX 1080 Ti GPUs; the 3 seeds of a configuration shared one GPU, \texttt{results/e3/compute.json}), and the CIFAR-10 test split is used only for the counts and the probe score.
